# modules.cpp SD cards

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other specifications. This document specifies mm.sdcard, an
SD card as an mm.fs BlockDevice in SPI mode (SpiCard) and in its native 4-bit
SD mode (SdioCard), and mm.sdcard.socket, the board-bound interface through
which a board publishes its TF socket. All exist; both card classes are
tested on the host against model cards, and the socket boards build
sd-smoke. Behaviour on hardware -- a card read and written on a board, and
for SdioCard the Pico's PIO timing at 25 MHz -- remains a
hardware-qualification requirement.

docs/modules-fs.mdy owns the BlockDevice seam and mm.fs.fat, which mounts
what this module presents. docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy owns the SPI, GPIO,
and sdio facilities it drives.

# Separation of responsibilities

- mm.mcu owns SPI transfers and the chip-select GPIO.
- mm.sdcard owns the SD protocol: identification, the CSD, addressing,
  commands, data tokens, and CRCs.
- A socket provider owns the pins.
- A board profile states that the socket is there.

# mm.sdcard

Opening fence
struct SpiWiring {
    mm::mcu::SpiConfiguration spi;    // clock, MOSI (CMD), MISO (DAT0); baud ignored
    unsigned int chip_select_gpio;    // DAT3
    unsigned long data_baud = 25'000'000;
};

class SpiCard final : public mm::fs::BlockDevice {
public:
    explicit SpiCard(SpiWiring wiring);
    Status geometry(BlockGeometry&);
    Status read(std::uint64_t block, std::span<std::byte>);
    Status write(std::uint64_t block, std::span<const std::byte>);
    Status sync();
};

std::uint8_t crc7(std::span<const std::byte>);
std::uint16_t crc16(std::span<const std::byte>);
Closing fence

The card initialises on first use. Identification follows the SD
Association's Physical Layer Simplified Specification for SPI mode:

1. SPI at 400 kHz, mode 0, most significant bit first; chip select an output,
   high; 80 clocks of 0xFF with it high.
2. CMD0 with its CRC, up to ten times, until R1 is idle (0x01). No answer is
   TransportError: there is no card.
3. CMD8 with argument 0x1AA. A card answering it illegal is version 1 and
   standard capacity; one answering it must echo the voltage and the check
   pattern, or the card is Unsupported.
4. CMD55 and ACMD41 -- with HCS for a version 2 card -- every millisecond
   until R1 is 0, for at most a second (Timeout). Any other R1 means the card
   is not an SD card: Unsupported.
5. For a version 2 card, CMD58; the OCR's CCS bit says the card is block
   addressed (SDHC, SDXC) rather than byte addressed (SDSC).
6. CMD59 with argument 1, turning the card's CRC checking on.
7. For a byte-addressed card, CMD16 for 512-byte blocks.
8. CMD9 and its 16-byte CSD: structure 1 gives (C_SIZE + 1) x 1024 blocks;
   structure 0 gives (C_SIZE + 1) x 2^(C_SIZE_MULT + 2) x 2^READ_BL_LEN / 512.
   Any other structure is Unsupported.
9. SPI at data_baud.

Every command frame carries its CRC7, and R1 is accepted within ten bytes.
Before every command but CMD0 the driver waits up to 500 ms for the card to
release its data line. A read is CMD17, or CMD18 then CMD12 and a busy wait,
each block taken after its 0xFE start token (within 200 ms; an error token is
TransportError) and checked against its CRC16. A write is CMD24 with token
0xFE, or CMD25 with token 0xFC per block and the 0xFD stop token, each block
followed by its CRC16 and a data response that must be accepted (0bxxx00101),
then a busy wait, and the write is confirmed with CMD13, whose two status
bytes must be zero.

Reads and writes take whole 512-byte blocks inside the card; a partial,
empty, or out-of-range transfer is BadArgument before the bus is touched.
Chip select is low only inside a transaction, followed by one byte of clocks
when it rises so the card releases MISO, so the bus can be shared. Any
failure inside a transaction forgets the card, and the next call starts again
at step 1: a card pulled and put back, or swapped, is found again.

# SdioCard: SD mode

Opening fence
struct SdioWiring {
    unsigned int instance = 0;
    unsigned int clock_gpio = 0;
    unsigned int command_gpio = 0;
    unsigned int data0_gpio = 0;          // D0; D1 to D3 are the next three GPIOs
    unsigned long data_clock_hz = 25'000'000;
};

class SdioCard final : public mm::fs::BlockDevice {
public:
    explicit SdioCard(SdioWiring wiring);
    Status geometry(BlockGeometry&);
    Status read(std::uint64_t block, std::span<std::byte>);
    Status write(std::uint64_t block, std::span<const std::byte>);
    Status sync();
    unsigned long data_clock_hz() const;  // what the platform gave; 0 until identified
};

bool csd_block_count(std::span<const std::byte> csd, std::uint64_t& blocks);
Closing fence

SdioCard speaks the card's native protocol over mm.mcu's sdio facility,
which frames commands, checks response and data CRCs, and waits out busy, so
this class holds only the card protocol. It initialises on first use:

1. sdio_configure on the wiring, four data lines; sdio_clock at 400 kHz; 80
   idle clocks.
2. CMD0, which takes no response.
3. CMD8 with 0x1AA. Silence is a version 1, standard-capacity card; an answer
   must echo the check pattern, or the card is Unsupported.
4. CMD55 and ACMD41 with the 2.7 V to 3.6 V window -- and HCS for a version 2
   card -- every millisecond until the OCR's busy bit says ready, for at most
   a second (Timeout). Silence to the first CMD55 is an empty socket,
   TransportError. The OCR's CCS bit says the card is block addressed.
5. CMD2 for the CID, CMD3 for the card's RCA, CMD9 addressed by it for the
   CSD, whose capacity csd_block_count decodes as SpiCard does.
6. CMD7 to select the card, ACMD6 with 2 for the 4-bit bus, and for a
   byte-addressed card CMD16 for 512-byte blocks.
7. sdio_clock at data_clock_hz.

A read is CMD17, or CMD18 for at most the facility's guaranteed eight blocks
followed by CMD12; a longer read is several. The facility takes a read buffer
on a four-byte boundary only, so a read into any other goes a block at a time,
CMD17 each, through a 512-byte buffer the SdioCard holds. A write is CMD24, or CMD25 for
any number of blocks followed by CMD12, then CMD13, whose status must show
no error. Every R1 is checked for the card status error bits. Any failure
forgets the card, and the next call identifies it again from CMD0.

SdioCard and SpiCard present the same BlockDevice, so a socket provider
chooses the transport and nothing above it changes: FAT mounts either.

# mm.sdcard.socket

Opening fence
namespace mm::sdcard::socket {
class Provider { virtual mm::fs::BlockDevice& card(); };
void set_provider(Provider&);
Provider& selected_provider();
mm::fs::BlockDevice& card();
}
Closing fence

A platform-interface: a board binds a provider that owns an SpiCard on its
socket's pins and answers it from card(), so a portable program writes
mm::fs::fat::mount("/sd", mm::sdcard::socket::card()) and names no pin. With
no provider bound, card() answers a device that is Unsupported throughout,
and a program reaching the interface is unavailable on that board.

Opening fence
provider                               socket wiring                      boards
platform.geek.sdcard                   SPI0 GP18/19/20, CS GP23           rp2040_geek, rp2350_geek
platform.rp2350_touch_lcd_154.sdcard   SPI0 GP18/19/20, CS GP23           rp2350_lcd_154, rp2350_touch_lcd_154
platform.pizero.sdcard                 SDIO CLK GP30, CMD GP31, D GP40-43 rp2350_pizero, rp2350_pizero_usb_host
platform.rp2350_touch_lcd_28.sdcard    SDIO CLK GP19, CMD GP20, D GP21-24 rp2350_touch_lcd_28
platform.linux.storage.socket          SPI0 GPIO 18/19/20, CS GPIO 23     storage-linux-x86_64, storage-linux-aarch64
Closing fence

The PiZero's and the RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8's sockets are SdioCards, in 4-bit SD
mode at 25 MHz; the GEEK and LCD 1.54 sockets stay SpiCards, whose wiring
hardware SPI reaches.

The storage-linux boards' socket holds the emulated card of
docs/modules-linux-storage.mdy, on the GEEK's wiring.

The RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8's socket is on GP19 to GP24, where GP19 is an SPI TX
position and GP20 an RX one, so hardware SPI cannot drive it; SD mode over PIO
is its only transport.

# apps/sd-smoke

sd-smoke mounts the socket's card at "/sd" with mm.fs.fat, runs the
mm.fs.conformance checks in a scratch directory, prints the counts and the
first failure, and unmounts. It never formats: a card holding no FAT volume
exits 2 and is left alone. An empty socket skips. scripts/build-sd.sh builds
it, by default for rp2040_geek.

# Tests

tests/mm/sdcard drives SpiCard against a byte-level model of an SD card in
SPI mode behind a fake mm.mcu platform. The model parses command frames,
checks CRC7 on CMD0 and CMD8 and on everything once CMD59 enables it, answers
CMD0, CMD8, CMD55 and ACMD41, CMD58, CMD59, CMD16, CMD9, CMD17, CMD18 with
CMD12, CMD24, CMD25 with its tokens, and CMD13, checks write CRC16s, and can
be an SDHC card, a version 2 SDSC card, or a version 1 one. The tests cover
the CRCs against the specification's values, SDHC identification and its
transcript, the 400 kHz and data clocks, block round trips single and
multiple, byte addressing on SDSC, arguments refused before the bus, a
missing card, one that never leaves idle, a corrupt read and recovery, a card
pulled mid-read and put back, chip select between transactions, and the
socket fallback. Dropping the CRC16 check, or always block addressing, makes
them fail.

tests/mm/sdcard also drives SdioCard against a command-level model of a card
in SD mode behind a fake platform implementing the sdio facility: SDHC
identification and its transcript, RCA addressing, the 4-bit switch and the
two clocks, single and multiple block round trips with reads split at eight
blocks and stopped by CMD12, byte addressing and CMD16 on both SDSC
versions, arguments refused before the bus, an empty socket, a card that
never leaves busy, a data CRC failure and a rejected write each forgetting
the card, a platform without the facility, and the CSD decode. The Pico
facility itself -- its PIO programs and DMA -- is exercised only on
hardware.

tests/mm/linux also drives SpiCard, unmodified, against the storage-linux
emulator's card through its mm.mcu bus: SDHC and both SDSC kinds, single and
multiple blocks landing at the right bytes of the store, and the emulator's
faults -- removal and reinsertion, write protection, a corrupt read, and a
write failing partway -- each reaching the driver as TransportError and
recovered from.

# apps/socket-smoke

socket-smoke works on mm.sdcard.socket's card below any file system: it
prints the geometry, then saves the last block, writes a pattern over it,
reads it back, and restores it. scripts/build-socket.sh builds it, by default
for storage, the emulated card, where --run runs it.

# Acknowledgements

The Pico implementation of the sdio facility learned its design from carlk3's
no-OS-FatFS-SD-SDIO-SPI-RPi-Pico (https://github.com/carlk3/no-OS-FatFS-SD-SDIO-SPI-RPi-Pico),
licensed under the Apache License 2.0, whose SDIO part in turn derives from
ZuluSCSI's firmware: a PIO state machine that drives the clock as side-set
while it shifts commands and responses, others that move the four data lines,
DMA to keep up with them, and CRC16 checked on every line. The programs,
framing, and code in platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/ext/adapter.c are this
project's own; nothing is copied from either project, and the acknowledgement
is recorded there as well.
